/-!
# Stalkwise tests for regularity of a sequence of global sections

Let `X` be a ringed space and let `f₀, …, f_{r-1}` be global sections of its structure sheaf.
Several constructions attached to such a sequence — the quotient of `O_X` by the ideal sheaf
generated by `f₀, …, f_{i-1}`, multiplication by `fᵢ` on that quotient, the Koszul complex of
the map `O_X^r ⟶ O_X` given by the `fᵢ`, and the canonical quasi-regularity morphism
`(O_X/J)[T₁, …, T_r] ⟶ ⨁_d J^d/J^{d+1}` — are *local*: they are computed at a point `x` by the
corresponding commutative-algebra construction applied to the sequence of germs
`germSequence X f x` over the local ring `O_{X,x}`.

This file proves the four transport statements that make those reductions available. Each is
phrased for an arbitrary *presentation* of the sheaf in question, given by the data that
characterizes it — for the quotient, a sectionwise map from the structure sheaf which commutes
with restriction, is locally surjective and has the prescribed ideal as kernel; for the Koszul
complex, a sectionwise identification of its homogeneous pieces with the exterior powers of
`O_X(U)^r` taking exterior products of the free generators to wedges of standard basis vectors,
under which the differential is the ring-level Koszul differential of the restricted sequence.
So no particular construction of these sheaves is presupposed: a statement proved here applies
to any sheaf satisfying the characterization.

## Main definitions

* `germSequence X f x` is the sequence of germs of `f` at `x`, and `resSequence X f U` its
  restriction to an open set.
* `precedingGermIdeal X f i x` is the ideal of the stalk `O_{X,x}` generated by the germs of
  the sections strictly preceding `fᵢ`.
* `quotientPresheafHom` assembles a sectionwise ring map commuting with restriction into a
  morphism of presheaves of commutative rings, and `stalkQuotientHom` is the ring
  homomorphism it induces on stalks at a point.
* `polynomialQuasiRegularCanonicalMap f` is the ring-level quasi-regularity map with its
  source written literally as the polynomial algebra `(R/J)[T₁, …, T_r]`.
* `wedgeBasis A r n` is the canonical basis of `⋀ⁿ(Aʳ)`, indexed by the `n`-element subsets of
  `Fin r`; `extBase ρ r n` is change of base ring on `⋀ⁿ(·ʳ)` along a ring homomorphism `ρ`.
* `germHom` and `resHom` are the germ and restriction maps on sections of a presheaf of
  `O_X`-modules, bundled as additive maps, and `abVal` retypes such a presheaf as a presheaf of
  abelian groups on the carrier of `X`.
* `toStalk` is the comparison from the degree-`n` part of the ring-level Koszul complex of the
  germ sequence to the stalk of the degree-`n` piece, and `stalkEquiv` is it as a linear
  equivalence. `stalkD` is the map induced on stalks by the sheaf-level differential, and
  `stalkComplex` the resulting complex of stalks.

## Main results

* `exists_stalkRingEquiv_quotient`: the stalk at `x` of a sheaf quotient of `O_X` by the ideal
  sheaf generated by `f₀, …, f_{i-1}` is canonically `O_{X,x} ⧸ precedingGermIdeal X f i x`,
  the isomorphism being specified by its action on germs of local sections.
* `germ_mul_eq_germSequence_mul`: under that identification, the endomorphism induced by
  multiplication by `fᵢ` becomes multiplication by the class of the germ of `fᵢ`.
* `isIso_iff_bijective_polynomialQuasiRegularCanonicalMap`: a morphism of sheaves of
  commutative rings whose stalks are the ring-level quasi-regularity maps is an isomorphism
  if and only if every one of those ring-level maps is bijective.
* `koszulD_ιMulti`: the alternating-sum formula for the Koszul differential on a wedge of
  vectors; `extBase_koszulD`: the Koszul differential of a sequence commutes with change of
  base ring.
* `stalkEquiv` and `stalkEquiv_symm_germHom_smul_wedgeSection`: the stalk of the degree-`n`
  piece is `⋀ⁿ(O_{X,x}^r)`, and the comparison sends the germ of a scalar times an exterior
  product of the free generators to the germ of the scalar times the corresponding wedge of
  standard basis vectors. This includes the unit in degree zero and the individual generators
  in degree one.
* `stalkHom_toStalk`: under that comparison the map induced on stalks by the sheaf-level
  differential is the ring-level Koszul differential of the germ sequence, and
  `seqComplexIsoStalkComplex` assembles this into an isomorphism of chain complexes between
  `Koszul.seqComplex (germSequence X f x)` and the complex of stalks.

## Implementation notes

The statements about stalks are necessarily in a single universe. Mathlib's
`TopCat.Presheaf.stalk` is available for `X : TopCat.{v}` and a presheaf valued in a category
whose hom universe is that same `v`; since `CommRingCat.{u}` is a `Category.{u}`, the carrier
of the ringed space must be a `TopCat.{u}`. Thus a stalk statement about `X : RingedSpace.{u₁, u₂}`
typechecks only for `u₁ = u₂`, and `RingedSpace.{u}` is forced here, not chosen. The predicate
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsLocallyInPrecedingIdeal` that the quotient hypotheses are phrased with
mentions no stalk and is therefore stated, in its own module, for `RingedSpace.{u, v}`.

The presentation data is taken as explicit hypotheses rather than bundled into a structure.
Three reasons: the theorems then apply to any sheaf satisfying the characterization, including
ones constructed later; the multiplication endomorphism is needed only as a family of
functions on sections, which is the weakest hypothesis under which the conclusion holds (in
particular it need not be assumed additive, natural, or a sheaf morphism); and no structure
declared here can collide with one declared by the file that eventually constructs these
sheaves.

`quotientPresheafHom` is where the sectionwise data becomes categorical. Naturality is assumed
only for the restriction morphisms `homOfLE h`, which is no loss: `Opens X` is a partial order,
so every morphism of `(Opens X)ᵒᵖ` is of that form, and `homOfLE_leOfHom` converts.

The kernel computation in `exists_stalkRingEquiv_quotient` runs in both directions and each
needs a different part of the hypotheses. That a germ killed by the stalk map lies in
`precedingGermIdeal` uses local generation plus `TopCat.Presheaf.germ_eq`. The converse uses
`AlgebraicGeometry.isLocallyInPrecedingIdeal_self`: a section `f j` with `j < i` lies in the
preceding ideal already over `⊤`, with the obvious coefficient vector, so its germ is killed.

Change of base ring on exterior powers (`extBase`) is defined *in the canonical basis* rather
than through a universal property. This is a choice, and it is the one that makes the whole
comparison elementary: the index type `Set.powersetCard (Fin r) n` does not depend on the ring,
so no restriction of scalars and no `Algebra` instance is needed to compare exterior powers over
`O_X(U)` and over `O_{X,x}`. That the basis-wise definition is the geometrically correct map is
`extBase_ιMulti`, proved by observing that the coordinates of a wedge of vectors in the
canonical basis are determinants of coordinate matrices (`exteriorPower.ιMultiDual_apply_ιMulti`)
and that determinants commute with ring homomorphisms (`RingHom.map_det`). The same observation
gives `extBase_id` and `extBase_comp`, i.e. functoriality.

The Koszul side of the comparison needs the differential in a form that survives change of base
ring. That is `Koszul.contraction_ιMulti`: contraction of a wedge of `n + 1` vectors is the usual
alternating sum. Transported to the exterior power it becomes `koszulD_ιMulti`, and with that
`extBase_koszulD` is a term-by-term comparison, so the sheaf-level argument never has to expand
the Koszul differential in coordinates.

The stalk comparison is constructed as a map *out of* the ring-level Koszul module, not into it:
`toStalk` is `Module.Basis.constr` applied to the germs of the exterior products of generators.
Defining it in this direction avoids building a cocone over the neighbourhoods of `x`, and the
whole content is then one lemma, `toStalk_extBase`: the comparison intertwines change of base
ring along the germ map with passage to germs. Bijectivity, the characterizing formula on germs
of scalar multiples of exterior products, and compatibility with the differentials are each a
short consequence. Injectivity is where `exists_extBase_eq` is used: the canonical basis is
finite, so finitely many coefficients can be realized over a single neighbourhood, obtained as
an `iInf` of opens (`Opens.coe_iInf` needs finiteness of the index, which holds).

Two typing subtleties arise for presheaves of modules. A presheaf of modules `M` carries two
spellings of the same group of sections, `M.obj (op U)` and `(abVal X M).obj (op U)`; they are
equal, but the `AddCommMonoid` instances Lean finds at the two spellings are not interchangeable
by `rw`, so `map_sum` fails on a sum formed at the wrong one. Germ and restriction are therefore
bundled as `AddMonoidHom`s out of `M.obj (op U)` (`germHom`, `resHom`), to which `map_sum`
applies. Second, `germHom` is only additive, not linear — the scalars act through different
rings on source and target — so it is used with `germHom_smul` rather than `map_smul`, which
would require an action of `O_X(U)` on the stalk.

Linearity of the induced map on stalks (`stalkD`) is *derived*, not proved by hand: the
comparison is a linear bijection and `stalkHom_toStalk` identifies the induced map with the
Koszul differential conjugated by it, so linearity, `d ∘ d = 0`, and the complex structure all
follow. Consequently `stalkComplex` is built from the genuine stalk maps of the given
differential, and `seqComplexIsoStalkComplex` is a statement about those rather than about a
complex constructed to make it true.

The comparison is stated for `PresheafOfModules (X.presheaf ⋙ forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)`
rather than for `SheafOfModules`. No sheaf condition is used anywhere: everything is a statement
about the filtered colimit defining the stalk, so the presheaf-level hypothesis is the correct
generality, and a sheaf of modules satisfies it.

